  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 240 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nAhead of a Pacific upper level low a few embedded shortwave impulses \nin the southwesterly flow aloft are anticipated to pass through the \narea. The best resolved of these shortwave troughs is anticipated \nThursday and will be attended by an impressive fetch of monsoonal \nmoisture. Ensemble PWAT values are progged to be near or just \noutside of the climate maximum. Widspread 1 to 1.5 inch PWAT values \ncould lead to proficient rainfall potential. GFS forecast soundings \nfor some spots in the NE Panhandle feature a near saturated profile \nwith an impressive depth to the warm cloud layer for this area. The \nCAPE values are rather paltry however. At the surface a weak lee \ncyclone may develop and move NE over the Panhandle locations. Behind \nthis cyclone cooler air filters in to drive afternoon temperatures a \nfew degrees lower than Wednesday. The WPC has a marginal risk of \nexcessive rainfall over our area. Fog is a possibility in the \nmorning hours east of I-25.\n\nFriday continues the cloudy and wet trend while the Pacific trough \ncontinues to inch eastward. The fetch of monsoonal moisture \ncontinues with widespread light showers and thunderstorms. The near \nsurface layer does dry out limiting greater rainfall potential. The \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall continues in the latest outlook.\n\nThis weekend the trough begins to flatten and decay as it nears our \nneck of the woods. Continued shower and storm activity in the \nafternoon and evening hours is likely to continue, but by this point \nthe monsoonal flow could be largely shut off with us on the dry \nwestern side of the trough. Southwesterly flow continues in the\nvery long term with more embedded shortwave troughs which could\nprovide a stormy start to the work week.",
